// constants-and-immutability.fol — application entry file
// Spec: language-ref.md > Constants and Immutability
// Grammar: annotation on a variable-declaration (@co.dap.const, @co.dap.final)
//
// FoLang separates two properties that are often confused:
//
//     WHEN a value is known    →  @co.dap.const
//     WHETHER it can change    →  @co.dap.final
//
// Every @co.dap.const is also immutable. The reverse does not hold.

// --- a compile-time constant ----------------------------------------------
// The value is fixed AND known while compiling, so the compiler can substitute
// a literal for the name anywhere it appears.
@co.dap.const SIZE co.lang.int = 1024;
@co.dap.const GREETING co.lang.string = "hello";

// --- an immutable binding -------------------------------------------------
// The binding cannot be reassigned, but the value need not be known early: it
// may come from a call, a file, or user input.
@co.dap.final startedAt co.lang.int = co.sys.now();

// --- why the difference matters -------------------------------------------
//
//   | annotation     | cannot change | known at compile time | usable as index |
//   |----------------|---------------|-----------------------|-----------------|
//   | @co.dap.const  | yes           | yes                   | yes             |
//   | @co.dap.final  | yes           | not required          | no              |
//
// Only @co.dap.const may name an array size or a dependent type index, because
// those positions need a value the compiler can substitute.

Buffer co.lang.type = co.lang.int->([SIZE]);
buffer Buffer;   // OK   SIZE substitutes to 1024
vec    Vector(SIZE);            // OK   the same rule for dependent types

// An immutable binding is not enough, even though it never changes:
//
//     bad co.lang.int->([startedAt]);   // ERR immutable, but not constant
//     bad Vector(startedAt);            // ERR same reason
//
// See 09-types/dependent-index-rules.fol for the full index rule.

// --- @co.dap.final is the declaration-site form of immutability -----------
// It is the declaration-site spelling of the immutable object kind described
// by the object mutation policy; makeImmutable applies the same property to a
// value at run time.
